@node Overview, Installation, Top, Top
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@chapter Overview

  Zebra is free routing software that manages TCP/IP based routing
protocols.  IPv6 routing protocols are also supported.  Zebra is
distributed under GNU General Public License (@pxref{Copying}).
@iftex
This manual describes @code{zebra} and some other beasts.
@end iftex

  You can get the latest information from Zebra web page:
@url{http://www.zebra.org/}.

@menu
* What is Zebra::               What is Zebra?
* System architecture::         Zebra system architecture
* Mailing list::                Mailing list information.
* Bug report::                  How to report bug.
@end menu

@node What is Zebra, System architecture, Overview, Overview
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section What is Zebra

  Today, TCP/IP networks are covering all of the world.  The Internet
has been in deployed many countries, companies and at home.  If you
connect to the Internet you use TCP/IP routing functions.  Zebra is a
free TCP/IP routing software package.  A Zebra installed box works as a
dedicated router.  With Zebra your machine talks with other routers by
means of routing protocols and updates the kernel routing table. You can
dynamically change the configuration and view routing table information
from the Zebra terminal interface.

  For a small network or a stub network there is no need for routing
software.  The only thing you have to do is to set up the interfaces and
put a few command about static route and/or default route.  But if the
network is rather large or if the network structure changes frequently,
you will need dynamic routing protocols such as RIP, OSPF or BGP.  Zebra
is for you

  Below is the list of currently supported routing protocol RFCs.

@table @samp
@item RFC1771
A Border Gateway Protocol 4 (BGP-4). Y. Rekhter & T. Li. March 1995.
@item RFC1997
BGP Communities Attribute. R. Chandra, P. Traina & T. Li. August 1996.
@item RFC2080
RIPng for IPv6. G. Malkin, R. Minnear. January 1997.
@item RFC2283
Multiprotocol Extensions for BGP-4. T. Bates, R. Chandra, D.  Katz,
Y. Rekhter. February 1998.
@item RFC2328
OSPF Version 2. J. Moy. April 1998.
@item RFC2453
RIP Version 2. G. Malkin. November 1998.
@item RFC2545
Use of BGP-4 Multiprotocol Extensions for IPv6 Inter-Domain Routing. P. Marques, F. Dupont. March 1999.
@end table

@page
@node System architecture, Mailing list, What is Zebra, Overview
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section System Architecture

  Traditional routing software are made as a one process program which
provides all of the routing protocol functionalities as a whole.  Zebra
takes different approach, it is made from collection of some daemons.
There are some protocol specific daemons and a kernel routing table
manager.

  @code{ripd} handles RIP protocol. @code{ospfd} is a daemon which
supports OSPF version 2.  @code{bgpd} supports BGP-4 protocol.  For
changing the kernel routing table and for redistribution between
different routing protocols, there is @code{zebra} daemon.  There is no
need for these daemons to be running over the same machine.  This
architecture makes new possibility of the routing system.  It is pretty
easy adding new routing protocol daemon to the system.

@example
@group
+----+  +----+  +-----+  +-----+
|bgpd|  |ripd|  |ospfd|  |zebra|
+----+  +----+  +-----+  +-----+
                            |
+---------------------------|--+
|                           v  |
|  UNIX Kernel  routing table  |
|                              |
+------------------------------+

    Zebra System Architecture
@end group
@end example

  Zebra use a multi-thread mechanism when it runs over a multi-thread
supported kernel.  But at this moment thread library which comes with
GNU/Linux or FreeBSD has some problem for running Zebra, so we don't use
thread those OSes, instead we use select system call for multiplexing.

  When zebra runs under GNU Hurd kernel it will act as a kernel routing
table itself.  Under GNU Hurd all TCP/IP services are provided by user
processes called @code{pfinet}.  Zebra will provide all the routing
selection mechanisms for the process.  This feature will be implemented
in the future.

